From d87f81331507830e2de0bf5e981fa948f4414bc0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:11:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 79/80] Save only the zero page temporaries a caller needs

A function saves the callee-saved temporaries it uses (BC_REG_TMP_SAVED on)
when it is entered and restores them when it returns, for its callers. Many
callers need few of those bytes, or none: in a NetHack workload mfndpos()
saves and restores 46 bytes on every call, 1,244 cycles, 10% of its time,
for a caller that keeps 16 of them over the call.

NativeCodeGenerator::ReduceTempSaves runs once every procedure is compiled,
before any call becomes a proxy, an outlined sequence or a bank stub. For
each direct call (a JSR or JMP to a procedure) it takes the callee-saved
bytes the caller reads after the call before it writes them: the native data
flow sets, plus the reach of copy loops (LDX #n / LDA zp,x ... / DEX / BPL),
which the sets do not follow; any other indexed access to the area makes the
caller need all of it. To that it adds what the caller's own callers need
from bytes the caller does not save itself (all of it, for a tail call). The
fixed point is what each procedure has to keep. A procedure then saves and
restores only those bytes, in up to three runs (runs closer than four bytes
are one), in the same slots of the same frame, with X only where its
prologue used X already (LOBJF_PRESERVE_REG_X); its zero page set grows by
the bytes it no longer keeps.

A procedure whose address is taken, that an interrupt can reach, or that
anything but a native procedure refers to keeps its full save, and so does
what it relies on from its callees. That includes main, whose caller, the
startup code, returns to basic, which needs basic's zero page as it was:
-xb says that the program does not return to basic (as -xz does, but with
the zero page laid out as usual), and main is then free too. A program with
byte code is left as it is.

-Ot turns the pass on, and it is part of -O2, -O3 and -Os; -Ot- turns it off.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
